| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Python | Detection logic, traffic analysis, and alert processing |
| TShark | Extracting network information from PCAP files |
| Streamlit | Web-based SOC dashboard |
| Pandas | Data processing and analysis |
| Altair | Interactive visualizations |
| Scikit-learn | Machine learning experiments |
| Git and GitHub | Version control and project development |

## Project structure

```text
DARKTRACE-LAB/
|
|-- dashboard/
|   |-- app.py
|
|-- scripts/
|   |-- detect_portscan_tshark.py
|   |-- detect_http_flood.py
|   |-- correlate_alerts.py
|   |-- train_multiclass.py
|   |-- ...
|
|-- models/
|   |-- isolation_forest.pkl
|   |-- isolation_forest_v2.pkl
|   |-- random_forest_multiclass.pkl
|   |-- random_forest_portscan.pkl
|
|-- data/
|-- alerts/
|-- main.py
|-- requirements.txt
|-- .gitignore
|-- README.md
```

The `data` directory contains local datasets and captures, while the `alerts` directory stores generated analysis results.

Large PCAP and dataset files are excluded from the Git repository.

## Running the project

### Requirements

- Python 3.13
- TShark (included with Wireshark)
- Python dependencies listed in `requirements.txt`
- The required CIC-IDS2017 PCAP capture

### Installation

Clone the repository:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/edzio-ghost/DARKTRACE-LAB.git
cd DARKTRACE-LAB
```

Create and activate a virtual environment on Windows:

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

Install the dependencies:

```powershell
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```

Download the required network capture from the [CIC-IDS2017 dataset](https://www.unb.ca/cic/datasets/ids-2017.html).

The current scripts use configured paths for the PCAP file and TShark executable. These paths may need to be adjusted for another computer.

Run the detection pipeline:

```powershell
python main.py
```

Launch the dashboard:

```powershell
python -m streamlit run dashboard/app.py
```

The dashboard will normally be accessible at `http://localhost:8501`.

## Current limitations

DARKTRACE LAB is still an experimental project.

At this stage, it has several limitations:

- It analyzes recorded PCAP files rather than live traffic.
- It currently supports two rule-based detection techniques.
- Detection thresholds have not been validated across a sufficiently diverse set of captures.
- The machine learning models are not integrated into the main detection pipeline.
- The dashboard does not yet allow users to select arbitrary PCAP files.
- The system does not block traffic or prevent attacks.

These limitations are part of the ongoing development and testing process.

## Next steps

My current priorities are:

1. Allow users to select different PCAP captures directly from the dashboard.
2. Test the detectors on additional network captures and evaluate false positives.
3. Improve the information available for investigating individual incidents.
4. Add automated tests for the detection and correlation logic.
5. Reevaluate the machine learning models using more reliable validation methods.
6. Explore live network monitoring once the offline analysis pipeline is sufficiently stable.

## About the project

I am developing DARKTRACE LAB as part of my learning path in cybersecurity, with a particular interest in network security, intrusion detection, and penetration testing.

The project gives me an opportunity to work with real network captures, understand detection logic, investigate technical limitations, and improve my ability to build security tools rather than only use existing ones.

I plan to continue improving the system as I develop my skills.

## Dataset

The project uses CIC-IDS2017, published by the Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity.

Dataset: https://www.unb.ca/cic/datasets/ids-2017.html

The original dataset files are not included in this repository.

